Examples of UGT‐SuSy catalyzed glycosylation reactions. A) Schematic reaction of in vitro glycosylation of NPs using the UGT‐SuSy cascade. B) Biocatalytic synthesis of different UDP‐sugar donors from UDP and sucrose.[

] C) Examples of glycosylated NPs reported to be glycosylated by the UGT‐SuSy cascade.[

] GalE: UDP‐galactose‐4‐epimerase, UGDH: UDP‐glucose 6‐dehydrogenase, UXS: UDP‐xylose synthase, UGE: UDP‐glucose 4‐epimerase, and LDH: lactate dehydrogenase.

Examples of GH‐catalyzed glycosylation reactions. A) Schematic reaction for the in vitro glycosylation of NPs using the GHs with various sugar donors, including α‐glucose fluoride (α.‐GlcF).[

] B) Competing side activities that hinder GHs mediated glycosylation, such as sucrose hydrolysis and polymerization, leading to undesired polymerized glycosides.[

] C) Examples of NPs reported to be glycosylated by GHs.[

]

In vivo glycosylation of NPs using whole‐cell biocatalysis (aglycon added to the media) or de novo production (aglycon synthesized from a carbon source or a precursor). A) Carbon sources such as glucose, sucrose, and cellobiose are transported inside the cell[

] and converted to precursor glucose‐1‐phosphate (G1P) using hexokinase (HK), phosphoglucomutase (PGM), sucrose phosphorylase (SP), or cellobiose phosphorylase (CP).[

] UDP‐Glc recycling is achieved through UGT, Nucleoside‐diphosphate kinase (NDK), and UDP‐glucose pyrophosphorylase (UGP) with G1P as a key intermediate. UDP‐Glc can also be further converted to other UDP‐sugars in a nucleotide sugar transformation module (NST).[

] B) Whole‐cell glycosylation using UGT‐SuSy cascade and a sucrose permease (SPe).[

] C) Examples of in vivo glycosylated NPs, highlighting the diversity of sugar moieties such as glucose, galactose, and rhamnose.[

] Arrows with red circles indicate the knockdown of genes of competing pathways. LacY: lactose permease, G6P: glucose‐6‐phosphate, and UTP: uridine triphosphate.
